REAL IMPROVEMENT: 91.4% -> 97.6% (23 residual rows down to 15). This is new structural work, not a permutation.

The unlock: retail keeps a 16-byte stack slot at 0x8 and assembles the pixel as a HALFWORD into it, then writes the three colour bytes at slot+4/+5/+6 and returns the 32-bit word at slot+8 (read back as "lwz r3, 0xc(r1)" at the epilogue). The prior 91.4% body used a bare "u32 result" with a "u8 *rgba" alias, which MWCC lays out differently.

Key evidence that forced the shape: retail case 4 emits TWO "sth" to the SAME offset 0x8(r1) - first "(u16)(lo << 8)", then the assembled pixel - and the three colour stores go to 0xc/0xd/0xe, NOT 0xb/0xc/0xd. Only a union {u8 bytes[8]; u16 halfwords[4]; u32 words[2];} reproduces that, and the return must be slot.words[1], not the whole union.

Spelled as:
    union { u8 bytes[8]; u16 halfwords[4]; u32 words[2]; } slot;
    case 4:
        lo = pixels[offset]; hi = pixels[offset + 1];
        slot.halfwords[0] = (u16)(lo << 8);                      // first sth
        pixel = (u16)((hi & 0xFF) | ((lo << 8) & 0xFFFF));
        slot.halfwords[0] = pixel;                               // second sth
        slot.bytes[4] = ...r; slot.bytes[5] = ...g; slot.bytes[6] = ...b;
        break;
    case 5: memcpy(slot.bytes, pixels + offset, 2); pixel = slot.halfwords[0]; ...
    return slot.words[1];

MEASURED DEAD ENDS (do not repeat): hoisting a "u8 *slot_ptr = pixels + offset" local above lo/hi to try to move retail's "add r3, r6, r0" into r3 -> 63.2% (-34); dropping the "#include rel/customize/emblem.h" -> 63.2%; the redundant "(u32)" cast on the first colour expression -> no change (97.6%); writing the colour bytes as a separate 4-byte struct -> 59.3% and 62.4%.

REMAINING 15 ROWS: pure register permutation inside case 4's colour extraction. Retail colours them r3 (->0xc), r0 (->0xd), r5 (->0xe) in source order r,g,b; mine lands r5/r3/r0. Note r0 is a volatile scratch holding the MIDDLE value, which means retail materialises g last despite storing it in the middle. That is an allocation-degree fact, not a source-order one.

